<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Fae Clans

''Humanity stands as one of the fae clans of Elyncia.'' Not a separate species that arrived among
the fae, and not a rival kind — ''a clan'', filed where elves, dwarves, goblins, insect-kin, and
the rest are filed. Every argument about who belongs on this world runs ''inside'' the fae
taxonomy, never across its border, because no such border was ever drawn.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-elder-residents>>

!! The Elder Residents ~ whose world this is

''The Gods have always been here.'' Not visitors, not later arrivals, not powers who found Elyncia
after she was made — the gods and the ''immortal fae lords'' have shared this world from the
beginning, and the fae lords are the elder clans' own heads. Divinity and faerie nobility stand as
co-native to Elyncia, and neither one holds a deed the other lacks.

''Gaia was the summer home.'' Many of them kept a second residence on Elyncia's sister world and
went there as one goes to a summer house — for the season, for the company, for reasons of their
own. Traffic ran both ways along the void-web for as long as the void-web held.

''The First Breaking closed the summer road.'' When the web shattered at YOLD 1, the powers who had
kept two homes were reduced to one, and Gaia was left with the memory of visitors who stopped
coming. What Gaia's own histories record as gods withdrawing from the world reads, from this side,
as ''the road home being cut''.

''Which inverts the obvious reading of the dream-mirror.'' Elyncia does not echo Gaia because she
copied her. The two worlds rhyme because ''the same powers walked both'', kept houses on both, and
left the same handprints on both — and then one of the two lost the company.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-arrivals>>

!! The Arrivals ~ how the human clan reached Elyncia

''The portals failed slowly.'' When the First Breaking tore the void-web joining Gaia to her
sister, the standing portals did ''not'' all fail at once. They went over an age — some closing
within the decade, some holding open across generations, some reopening in periods of high mana
long after everyone had stopped expecting them. Gaia's mages spent that whole failing age
''sending populations through'', in waves, from a Bronze Age collapsing on its own terms.

''And they arrived somewhere their gods already summered.'' The refugees came from a world their
powers had kept a house on for aeons — which is one reason the human clan reads as ''fae'' at all,
rather than as something the taxonomy had to make room for. The family had been visiting for a very
long time.

''So arrival ran staged, not singular.'' The human clan of Elyncia descends from an entire era of
refugees — different centuries, different cultures, different reasons for running — which is why
the modern populations read ''extremely multi-cultural'' and why no single origin-story serves
them all. Other demi-planes fed the same door. The clan was assembled in transit.

''What they took as their own.'' The human clan's Affinity runs ''Hearth & Ways'' — the two
domains the Lares themselves keep, the household and the road. A clan that arrived through
thresholds took the threshold as its birthright, and its sub-lineages carry the same signature:
''Changeling'', ''Frontierling'', ''Rainbowlander''. Crossroads people, all of them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-conceit>>

!! The Neo-Thracian Conceit

''The empire's founding vanity:'' that humans stood as ''the best of the fae clans''.

Read it exactly. Not //better than the fae// — the claim never denies the kinship, because denying
it would forfeit the argument. Neo-Thracian supremacism runs ''intra-fae'': the youngest clan,
arrived through a failing door, declaring itself the finest expression of the family that took it
in. That conceit built an empire, and the empire's magitech, bureaucracy, and eight-century golden
age all stood as the evidence it offered.

''What the other clans made of it'' runs from indulgence to open war, and the ''Faerie Court
Wars'' (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/elyncia/concepts/the-faerie-court-wars`) name the era where the argument
stopped being rhetorical.

''Where the conceit sits now:'' the Neo-Thracian Scions still claim imperial bloodlines in a Nexus
whose empire fell 492 years ago, and the claim still carries weight with people who cannot
remember why. A conceit outlives the thing it justified. That is generally what conceits are for.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-courts>>

!! Clans and Courts ~ not the same axis

''A clan names descent. A Court names allegiance.'' The old ''Summer'' and ''Winter'' Courts
gathered many clans each; the modern Courts — ''Seelie'', ''Unseelie'', ''Ouroborous'',
''Brimstone'', ''Crystal'', and others — gather them differently. A clan may scatter across every
Court; a Court holds whichever clans swore to it.

''Which is why the Ouroborous Court reads as it does:'' led by a mortal raised to Faerie Lord
status. A human-clan warlord holding a Faerie Court surprises nobody who has the taxonomy right,
and reads as an outrage to anyone who does not.

<<~/ahu>>

